metal2vulkan runtime: no-copy buffers and IOSurface textures on a device that imports no host memory (Scion)

Upstream:  metal2vulkan, https://github.com/steelbrain/metal2vulkan, at 720ef1ce9ae2a2068999a8675717418b9416283e, after
           AdrenoMetal's 16 patches to it and Scion's 0001-host-memory-not-coherent.patch; tools/metal/mk-metal2vulkan.sh applies
           them in that order, then this.
Licence:   GPL-2.0-or-later OR LGPL-3.0-or-later, metal2vulkan's (its LICENSE); this patch is a change to it and under the same
           terms. Nothing of it is in Scion's MIT or APSL files.
Author:    the HakiOS authors (Scion), 29 October 2026.

Why. A no-copy buffer (newBufferWithBytesNoCopy:), and with it every texture over an IOSurface (the frontend makes one over the
surface's pages), is an import of the caller's pages (VK_EXT_external_memory_host). gfxstream's guest driver for Scion imports
none, so every such buffer was refused (Unsupported, HostMemory), and every drawable with it.

What. A copy fallback, on the same two points as 0001's flushes:
- buffer_descriptor.rs: where the import is refused as Unsupported(HostMemory), the no-copy buffer is an ordinary Shared
  buffer of the device's that stands for the caller's bytes (buffer.rs, CopiedHost); its `contents` is the caller's pointer,
  as Metal's is, and the deallocator (kept apart while the import is tried) runs as the buffer goes.
- before a command buffer's first batch (with 0001's flush of the program buffers it uses): the caller's bytes of each such
  buffer it uses go into the buffer's own memory where they changed, compared 4 KiB at a time, and only those ranges are
  flushed;
- as it completes (with 0001's invalidate of what it wrote): each such buffer its tracked uses write (or it uses untracked) is
  invalidated whole and copied back into the caller's bytes where they changed. A buffer the GPU only reads is not copied
  back, so the host's writes to it meanwhile are kept.
- metal2vulkan_runtime::copied_host_counts(): bytes copied in and out, and the time each took.
A texture over such a buffer is the runtime's ordinary texture over a buffer; its uses are the buffer's.

Checked (tools/metal/m2v_test.c, tools/metal/metalvk-plugin-test.sh): through gfxstream's guest driver on its Mac twin, the
self-test's import step and the plug-in's newTextureWithDescriptor:iosurface:plane: (rendered into, read from the surface's
memory) pass; on lavapipe with the import hidden (HAKIOS_VK_NO_HOST_IMPORT=1), with and without host memory that is not
coherent (HAKIOS_VK_INCOHERENT=1), every check passes. A full-screen 1125x2436 BGRA8 surface rendered every frame costs, on
the Mac twin of gfxstream, 5.2 ms a frame more than a Private texture: 0.4 ms comparing, 0.65 ms copying 12 MB back, the rest
the invalidate through the driver.

